To test how a device can affect a company network, security professionals use penetration testing (pentesting) tools. These tools simulate how an attacker moves from a compromised device to the rest of the enterprise. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
The exact tools depend on what stage of the attack you are testing. [6, 7] 
## 1. Network Discovery & Scanning
Before an attacker can move laterally, they need to see what else is on the network. [8] 

* Nmap: The industry standard for network discovery and vulnerability scanning. It maps out active devices, open ports, and operating systems. [9, 10, 11, 12, 13] 
* Responder: A tool that listens for and intercepts local network broadcast protocols (like LLMNR and NBT-NS). It is used to steal network password hashes directly from the wire. [14, 15] 

## 2. Exploitation & Initial Access
These tools allow you to test if a device can be used to launch exploits against internal servers. [16, 17] 

* Metasploit Framework: A massive database of known exploits. It automates the process of targeting unpatched vulnerabilities found on other corporate machines. [18, 19, 20] 
* Impacket: A collection of Python classes for working with network protocols (especially Windows SMB). It is heavily used to execute commands remotely on other network systems. [21, 22, 23, 24] 

## 3. Post-Exploitation & Lateral Movement
Once inside, these frameworks simulate how a hacker controls the compromised device and expands their reach. [25, 26, 27] 

* Cobalt Strike / Brute Ratel: Advanced command-and-control (C2) frameworks used by red teams to simulate sophisticated, stealthy corporate network compromises. [28, 29, 30] 
* BloodHound: A visual tool that maps out Active Directory relationships. It shows exactly which user accounts or devices have a direct path to becoming a Domain Administrator (gaining full company control). [31, 32, 33] 
* Mimikatz: A tool used to extract plain-text passwords, hashes, and Kerberos tokens from the memory of a Windows device. [34, 35] 

## 4. Vulnerability Management

* Nessus / OpenVAS: Automated scanners that you run from the perspective of the device to see every missing patch and configuration flaw visible on the corporate network. [36] 

